00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:42.000
Very good, Tom. Very good. There are just our old crones and small children. Cackling. Do they have white eyeballs? Undoubtedly. But there's no men. Where are the men?
我们在关于比利时的那一集里谈到了德国人对法兰克-蒂尔厄尔（Franc de Ruhrers）即游击队的恐惧。但这处于不同的水平，因为塞尔维亚人，他们热爱。我的意思是，塞尔维亚人热爱游击队。所以从一开始，他们就发动了这场游击战。奥地利人脑海中的这种印象被以下事实加剧了：塞尔维亚人，甚至是他们的正规部队，因为他们没有留下任何制服，看起来就像强盗。他们有点，你知道，身上挂着弹药带。我的意思是，基本上，他们就是强盗。是的，差不多。

49
00:20:42.000 --> 00:21:10.000
We talked in the episode about Belgium about the Germans fear of Franc de Ruhrers of sort of partisans. But this is on a different level because the service, they love a. I mean, the Serbs love a partisan. And so from the very beginning, they waged this partisan campaign. And it's exacerbated in the minds of the Austrians by the fact that the Serbs, even their regular troops, because they don't have any uniforms left, they look like bandits. They've kind of got their, you know, sort of bandoliers of ammunition. I mean, basically, they are bandits. Yeah, pretty much.
所以他们会做的是，奥地利人穿过一个村庄，然后他们穿过去，走进树林里。此时，塞尔维亚人就会出击。他们从后面向所有人开枪，然后融入巴尔干的风景中。现在，奥地利人可不会逆来顺受。现在我们来到了东线的一个巨大特征，无论它在哪里发生，其实都一样。这就是对平民的杀戮。奥地利人在比利时让德国人看起来像，你知道，社会工作者之类的人。对吧。

50
00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:35.000
So what they will do is the Austrians will march through a village, and then they'll go through and they'll go off into the woods. And at this point, the Serbs will strike. They'll shoot them all from behind, and they'll melt away into the Balkan landscape. Now, the Austrians do not take this lying down. And now we come to a massive feature of the Eastern Front, no matter where it happens, actually. And this is the killing of civilians. The Austrians make the Germans in Belgium look like, you know, social workers or something. Right.
所以他们的战地指挥官是一个名叫利博里乌斯·里特·冯·弗兰克（Liborius, Ritter von Frank）将军的家伙。他当然是。他说，听着，塞尔维亚人无论如何都是糟糕透顶的人。既然我们受到平民的攻击，那我们就撕破脸皮了，忘掉国际法吧，它真的不适用。让我们直接，你知道，大开杀戒吧。所以他们立刻开始掠夺和焚烧这些村庄。他们射杀任何他们甚至有一点点认为与游击活动有关的人。现在，我必须说，在任何人开始为塞尔维亚人哭泣之前，

51
00:21:35.000 --> 00:22:03.000
So their field commander is a guy called General Liborius, Ritter von Frank. Of course he is. And he says, listen, the Serbs are terrible people anyway. Since we're under attack from civilians, like the gloves are off, forget about international law, doesn't really apply. Let's just, you know, go for it. So straight away they start looting and burning these villages. They shoot anybody that they think even remotely is connected with partisan activity. Now, I have to say, before anyone starts sort of weeping for the Serbs,
塞尔维亚人在三年前的巴尔干战争中确实让自己名誉扫地。我的意思是，他们在科索沃、马其顿等地表现得确实极其恶劣。他们种下了什么，现在就在一定程度上收获什么。我能问一下，在比利时，当德国人对平民犯下暴行时，这显然对德国的声誉是个坏消息，因此从长远来看对他们进行战争的能力也是个坏消息。但有人在关注巴尔干地区发生的事情吗？伦敦绅士俱乐部里是不是有一种轻描淡写的态度，认为这只是巴尔干地区人们常做的那种事？完全正确。

52
00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:41.000
the Serbs three years earlier had really disgraced themselves in the Balkan Wars. I mean, they had behaved really atrociously in Kosovo and Macedonia and so on. What they've sowed, they are now reaping to some extent. Can I just ask, in Belgium, when the Germans commit atrocities against civilian, This is obviously bad news for Germany's reputation and therefore in the long run for their ability to prosecute the war. But is anyone paying attention to what's going on in the Balkans? And isn't there a kind of a slight attitude in the gentlemen's clubs of London that this is just the kind of thing that people do in the Balkans? Yeah, totally right.
双重标准。同样的双重标准也适用于我们谈论俄国人在波兰和乌克兰的暴行时，例如。我的意思是，完全如此。基本上西方没有人关心。而且就他们在乎的程度而言，每当提起时，他们都会说，好吧，我的意思是，这种事情在那里一直都在发生。古老的仇恨。古老的仇恨，完全如此。

53
00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:57.000
Double standard. The same double standard applies when we're talking about the Russian atrocities in Poland and Ukraine, for example. I mean, exactly that. that basically nobody cares in the West. And insofar as they do care, whenever it comes up, they say, well, I mean, this kind of thing happens there all the time. Ancient hatreds. Agent hatreds, exactly.
事情就是这样。而且，尽管说了“塞尔维亚人种下了什么，现在就在收获什么”，即便如此，还是有一些非常、非常残酷的故事。这里有一个很好的例子。由奥地利人自己讲述。这是一个年轻的匈牙利军官亚历山大·帕拉瓦奇尼（Alexander Palavaccini）讲述的。8 月 17 日，他的部队纵队遭到来自玉米地的射击。他们派出了巡逻队，

54
00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:18.000
That's exactly what happens. And having said, oh, what the Serbs sowed there now reaping, even so, there are some really, really grim stories. So here's a good example. It's a told by the Austrians themselves. So this is from a young Hungarian officer called Alexander Palavaccini. So his column of troops was fired on from a cornfield on the 17th of August. And they sent out patrols.
这些巡逻队带回了 63 个人。他们说在田野附近发现了这些人。帕拉瓦奇尼声称，其中一些是被捕获的带有步枪的妇女和儿童。其中一人是一名被捕获带有手榴弹的正教会神父。他写道：一小时后，只能看到一个万人坑。为了不让我们的士兵听到枪声而感到不安，

55
00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:38.000
and these patrols came back with 63 people. They said they'd found hanging around the field. Some of these, Palavacchina claimed, were women and children caught with rifles. One of them was an Orthodox priest caught with grenades. And he wrote, An hour later, only a mass grave was visible. In order not to upset our soldiers by the sound of shooting,
这些人被刺刀刺死。据说那个神父的胡子被扯掉了。在经历了对他们的暴行之后，我们的人是如此愤怒。他非常严厉地描述了这一点，他没有任何后悔或内疚。他说，对我们的仇恨是无边无际的，每个人都是我们的敌人。我们不是在与一支 30 万人的军队作战，而是在与整个国家作战。多米尼克，据推测，神父拥有手榴弹、妇女和儿童拥有枪支的证据完全来自于开枪射杀他们的人。

56
00:23:38.000 --> 00:24:05.000
these people were bayoneted to death. The priest's beard had supposedly been ripped off. Our men were that angry after the atrocities committed against them. He describes this very starkly, he has no regrets or guilt. He says, the hatred for us is boundless and everyone is our enemy. We're not fighting against an army of 300,000, but against a whole nation. And Dominic, presumably, the evidence for the priest having the grenades and the women and children having guns comes exclusively from the people who've shot them.
当然，是的。谁能说这有多真实呢？而且奥地利人根本不试图掩盖这一点。他们实际上希望它是公开的。所以康拉德说，你知道，把这做得非常明显，因为我们想恐吓人们与我们合作。一周后的 8 月 24 日，另一位奥地利-匈牙利军官讲述了一个可怕的故事。他说，我遇到了一纵队 30 个人集合起来准备处决。他说他们站在那里时被踢、被鞭打之类的。

57
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:32.000
Of course, yes. Who's to say how true this is? And the Austrians do not try to cover this up at all. They actually want it to be public. So Conrad says, you know, make this very overt because we want to terrify people into collaborating with us. There's a terrible story told by another Austro-Hungarian officer a week later, the 24th of August. He says, I met a column of 30 people assembled for execution. He says they were being kind of kicked and whipped and whatnot while they were standing there.
他和另外几个人试图制止这样做的人，但不可能。他接着说，修道院后面的树林边缘有一个刑场。塞尔维亚人不得不自己挖坟墓。然后他们被按坐在坑前，每五个人一组被刺刀刺死，三个步兵各刺一人，这是一个可怕的景象。看到泥土堆在受害者身上，而有些人还在挣扎，甚至试图从坟墓里爬出来，看到一些人从坟墓里爬起来，真是太可怕了。我们的人表现得像野蛮人。

58
00:24:32.000 --> 00:25:01.000
He and a couple of others tried to restrain the people doing it, but it was impossible. And he goes on to say, there was an execution place at the edge of the woods behind the monastery. The Serbs had to dig their own graves. Then they were sat down in front of the pit and bayoneted five at a time, Three infantrymen stabbed each, a gruesome spectacle. It was terrible to see earth being heaped on the victims while some still lived, and indeed tried to climb out of the grave and to see some of those rising from the grave. Our men behaved like savages.
我无法忍受这景象，我把他们留在那儿了。所以，你知道，奥地利-匈牙利军官自己也为此感到困扰。他们在两周内大概处决了大约 3,000 人。天哪，是的。尽管有关于军乐队和蛋糕的一切说法，奥地利军队确实有黑暗的一面。所以本我（id）和自我一样在发挥作用。是的，西格蒙德·弗洛伊德会度过疯狂的一天。

59
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:23.000
I couldn't stand the sight and I left them to it. So, you know, Austro-Hungarian officers themselves are troubled by this. They probably executed about 3,000 people in two weeks. Oh dear, yeah. For all the stuff about the military bands and the cake, There is a kind of dark side to the Austrian army. So the id, the id as well as the ego is in play. Yeah, Sigmund Freud would have a field day.
与此同时，奥地利人一直在向塞尔维亚行军。他们有一个特定的目标，那就是瓦列沃（Vallevo）的一个铁路枢纽。路上有一座森林密布的山脊，叫做塞尔山（Mount Sir）。他们在 8 月 15 日的傍晚到达了山顶。他们精疲力竭。他们非常口渴。他们非常饥饿。那天晚上有一场巨大的雷暴。

60
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:44.000
Now, meanwhile, the Austrians have been marching into Serbia. They have a particular objective in mind, which is this railway junction at a place called Vallevo. And in the way, there is this kind of heavily forested ridge called Mount Sir. And they reached the top on the late afternoon in the 15th of August. They're knackered. They're very thirsty. They're very hungry. There's a massive thunderstorm that night.
与此同时，奥地利军队已经挺进塞尔维亚。他们有一个特定的目标，也就是瓦列沃的一个铁路枢纽。而在他们前进的路上，有一座树木茂密的的山脊，叫做切尔山。他们在8月15日的傍晚抵达了山顶。他们精疲力竭。他们又渴又饿。那天晚上还下了一场特大的雷阵雨。

61
00:25:44.000 --> 00:26:22.000
And in the middle of the thunderstorm, that's the problem. that's the point that the Serbs choose to strike back. And the result is total chaos. The Austrians are inexperienced. They panic. They start running away. They're kind of a huge mob of Austrians running away down this mountain. Unbelievably, they just keep running. And within four days, they've crossed the river again and they've gone back to Bosnia. So their first attempt to invade Serbia has been a ludicrous failure and a very bloody failure. The Serbs have lost 16,000 men. The Austrians have lost 30,000, including loads and loads of their officers. And in the eyes of the world, this is a terrible humiliation for them.
而正是在这场雷雨之中，问题出现了，塞尔维亚人正是在这个时候选择反击的。结果是一片混乱。奥地利军队缺乏经验，他们惊慌失措，开始逃跑。他们就像一大群乌合之众，顺着这座山逃命。难以置信的是，他们一直在逃跑。不到四天，他们又渡过河流，退回了波斯尼亚。因此，他们对塞尔维亚的第一次入侵是一场荒谬且血腥的惨败。塞尔维亚人损失了1.6万人，奥地利人损失了3万多，其中包括大量的军官。在全世界眼中，这对他们来说是奇耻大辱。

62
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:44.000
General Pottyurek has spent the whole time in Sarajevo, ironically. He says, oh, well, this wasn't my fault at all. I think it was the fault of our Czech troops, actually. They're very disloyal, they're poorly behaved the Czechs. By blaming the Czechs, he's able to cling on to his job. And straight away, he starts writing to Conrad and he says, I'd love to have another crack at the Serbs. You send me some more men and some more guns and whatnot.
讽刺的是，波蒂奥雷克将军在这期间一直待在萨拉热窝。他说，哦，这根本不是我的错。实际上，我认为这是我们捷克军队的错。捷克人非常不忠诚，表现很差。通过指责捷克人，他得以保住自己的饭碗。紧接着，他开始写信给康拉德，说，我很想再给塞尔维亚人一点颜色看看。你再派给我一些人马和火炮之类的。

63
00:26:44.000 --> 00:27:06.000
Conrad says, no, I don't think so. You've had your chance. The Pottyurek is well connected at court. He gets his friends to lobby the Emperor Franz Joseph. And eventually he's given permission to have another crack at the Serbs. So, at dawn on the 14th of September, he has a second go. Once again, they cross the rivers into Serbia from Bosnia. This time, the Serbs have dug trenches.
康拉德说，不，我看不必了。你已经有过机会了。波蒂奥雷克在朝廷里很有后台。他让他的朋友们去游说弗兰茨·约瑟夫皇帝。最终，他获得了再次打击塞尔维亚人的准许。于是，在9月14日黎明，他发起了第二次进攻。他们再次从波斯尼亚跨越河流进入塞尔维亚。这一次，塞尔维亚人已经挖好了战壕。

64
00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:41.000
And it degenerates quite quickly into kind of bloody hand-to-hand trench warfare. Austrians are thrown back. Oh, no. It will not quite as chaotic as before. They say, well, we'll have another go. So in November, they have attempt number three. Now, at this point, the Serbs are absolutely exhausted themselves, and they're running out of shells. They haven't got running out of artillery. They can't really rest their troops because they're running out to mem. So slowly, painfully, the Austrians are able to inch their way forward. And the Serbs have to abandon Belgrade, and they take up a new defensive line on a river called the Colobara, which is deep inside central Serbia. So at this point, the Austrian, the
战局很快恶化成血腥的近距离战壕战。奥地利人被击退了。哦，不。情况没有之前那么混乱。他们说，好吧，我们再试一次。所以在11月，他们进行了第三次尝试。此时此刻，塞尔维亚人自己也精疲力竭，炮弹也快打光了。他们的火炮弹药告罄。他们根本无法让部队休整，因为他们人手不足。所以，奥地利人缓慢而艰难地向前推进了一小步。塞尔维亚人不得不放弃贝尔格莱德，并在科卢巴拉河（一条深入塞尔维亚中部内陆的河流）建立了一条新的防线。所以在这个时候，奥地利……

65
00:27:41.000 --> 00:28:24.000
Austrians, foolishly, I think, Tom, decide to celebrate victory. They have premature victory celebrations. The emperor sends potty or echo message of congratulations. Lots of towns name him an honorary citizen. A street in Sarajevo is renamed in his honour. Wow, that's punchy. That is, yeah. The man is basically his foolishness has caused the whole thing. It's caused the First World War, yeah. However, it will surprise you to know that the Austrians are perhaps being a little bit premature. Because that guy who they'd released the bloke with a beard and emphysema, Radimir Putnik. His emphysema's got far worse under the pressure war, so he can't breathe. We will not surrender. Exactly. But even though he can't breathe, he's still a much better
汤姆，我认为奥地利人愚蠢地决定庆祝胜利。他们举行了过早的庆祝胜利活动。皇帝给波蒂奥雷克发去了贺电。许多城镇授予他荣誉市民称号。萨拉热窝的一条街道以他的名字重新命名。哇，这可真够讽刺的。确实是这样。这个人的愚蠢基本上导致了整件事情。是的，它引发了第一次世界大战。然而，你可能会感到惊讶的是，奥地利人或许高兴得有点太早了。因为那个被他们释放的人——那个留着胡子、患有肺气肿的人，拉多米尔·普特尼克。在战争的压力下，他的肺气肿恶化得厉害，以至于呼吸困难。我们绝不投降。没错。但尽管他呼吸困难，他依然是一个比任何奥地利指挥官都优秀得多的……

66
00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:52.000
commander than any of the Austrians. So he knows that the Austrians have now, they've gone on the attack, they've sustained massive casualties, more than 130,000 casualties. They haven't got any food, they're ravaged by lice, they're miles ahead of their supply lines, they've got no shoes, but their fancy uniforms are in rags. They're very miserable, and actually the weather has now changed. This is the thing about the Eastern Front. There's only two weather settings. It's either unbelievably suffocatingly hot, or you're dying of frostbite.
指挥官。所以他知道奥地利人现在已经发起进攻，他们蒙受了巨大的伤亡，伤亡人数超过13万人。他们没有食物，饱受虱子折磨，距离补给线有数英里远，没有鞋子，华丽的军服也变成了破布。他们苦不堪言，而且天气也变了。这就是东欧战场的特点。这里只有两种天气状况。要么热得令人窒息，要么你就要冻死了。

67
00:28:52.000 --> 00:29:31.000
So now it's snowing. And meanwhile, Putnik has done something amazing. He's arranged with the French to have 11,000 shells imported via Sononica, Thessaloniki. They're the wrong size for the Serbian gun, so every single shell has been taken apart and reassembled and transported to the front. So late November, Putnik rallies his forces. He's basically got all the Serbian men. That's 200,000 of them. He's got 400 guns. And he has a secret weapon. And what's that, Dominic? A secret weapon is a man. It's Petar Karajorjevich, the king of Serbia. The king of Serbia. And Dominic, just a wild guess here.
所以现在开始下雪了。与此同时，普特尼克做了一件了不起的事。他通过塞萨洛尼基（索伦卡）与法国人协商进口了1.1万枚炮弹。这些炮弹的尺寸不适合塞尔维亚的大炮，因此每一枚炮弹都被拆开、重新组装并运送到前线。所以在11月下旬，普特尼克召集了他的部队。他基本上集结了所有的塞尔维亚男丁，共计20万人。他拥有400门大炮。他还有一个秘密武器。多米尼克，那是什么？秘密武器是一个人。他就是塞尔维亚国王佩塔尔·卡拉乔杰维奇。塞尔维亚国王。多米尼克，我大胆猜一下。

68
00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:52.000
Yeah. From what I've previously learned about significant players in the early months of the First World War, I'm guessing he's very elderly, I'm guessing he's very melancholy, and I'm guessing that he has some terrible ailment, would I be correct? He's got rheumatism, but if you Google him, he really does have a superb moustache. And he's elderly? Yeah, he's 70. Of course.
嗯。根据我之前对第一次世界大战头几个月重要人物的了解，我猜他年纪很大，我猜他非常忧郁，我猜他患有某种可怕的疾病，我猜得对吗？他患有风湿病，但如果你在谷歌上搜索他，你会发现他确实留着一抹极棒的小胡子。而且他年纪很大？对，他70岁了。当然。

69
00:29:52.000 --> 00:30:29.000
Anyway, he tours the trenches, King Petar, and he says, my children, I have learned that some among your number tire of fighting, but I shall stay to the end. And a French reporter who is there said of the Serbs, I'm sure this is true. Each of them felt his soul gripped with a furious desire to seize victory or to die for their old uncle, who had stayed there among his children, in spite of his failing health, even as the cannons roared and the bullets whistled. I mean, it's interesting, isn't it, how it mirrors the pattern of what's happened on the Western Front, that the Germans overextend their supply lines. There's an elderly man who says we're going to fight.
不管怎样，国王佩塔尔巡视了战壕，他说：“我的孩子们，我得知你们当中有些人厌倦了战斗，但我将坚守到最后。”当时在场的一位法国记者对塞尔维亚人这样评价，我确信这是真的：“每个人都感到自己的灵魂被一种强烈的渴望所攫取，那就是为了他们的老叔叔夺取胜利或战胜而死。尽管健康状况不佳，这位老叔叔依然留在他的孩子们中间，任凭大炮轰鸣，子弹呼啸。”我的意思是，这很有趣，不是吗？它反映了西线发生的事情的模式，即德国人过度延伸了他们的补给线。有一个老人说我们要战斗。

